# Development loop

Never code first and document later. Architecture drives the code.

```
Idea
  → Engineering Notebook (requirements, architecture, interfaces)
  → Cursor implements exactly that subsystem
  → Test
  → Git Commit (one logical milestone)
  → Repeat
```

## Before a new subsystem

Spend time on the notebook (see `docs/SubsystemTemplate.md`): Purpose, Requirements, Inputs/Outputs, Failure modes, Performance goals, Test cases, Open questions.

Do **not** start with prompts like “write a queue.” First update the page (e.g. `docs/DurableQueue.md`), resolve open questions via ADR when needed, then prompt:

> Implement the &lt;Subsystem&gt; exactly as specified in the engineering notebook (`docs/&lt;Page&gt;.md`).

## Rules

1. Update `docs/` before writing new subsystem code.
2. Implement only what the notebook specifies—no silent scope expansion.
3. Test against the notebook’s test cases before committing.
4. Each commit is one logical milestone for that loop pass.
5. If tests change the design, update the notebook before the next implementation pass.
